"MasFACT: Continual Multi-Agent Topology Learning via Geometry-Aware Posterior Transfer (https://arxiv.org/pdf/2605.17361v1)\nciteturn41152view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 29; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn41146view0\",\"lineno\":882}); Total lines: 1757\nL855@P12: Transactions on Learning Technologies, 2025.\nL856@P12: [49] Junhao Zheng, Shengjie Qiu, Chengming Shi, and Qianli Ma. Towards lifelong learning of large\nL857@P12: language models: A survey. ACM Computing Surveys, 57(8):1–35, 2025.\nL858@P12: [50] Wanjun Zhong, Ruixiang Cui, Yiduo Guo, Yaobo Liang, Shuai Lu, Yanlin Wang, Amin Saied,\nL859@P12: Weizhu Chen, and Nan Duan. Agieval: A human-centric benchmark for evaluating foundation\nL860@P12: models. In Findings of the association for computational linguistics: NAACL 2024, pages\nL861@P12: 2299–2314, 2024.\nL862@P12: [51] Wanjun Zhong, Lianghong Guo, Qiqi Gao, He Ye, and Yanlin Wang. Memorybank: Enhancing\nL863@P12: large language models with long-term memory. In Proceedings of the AAAI conference on\nL864@P12: artificial intelligence, volume 38, pages 19724–19731, 2024.\nL865@P12: [52] Han Zhou, Xingchen Wan, Ruoxi Sun, Hamid Palangi, Shariq Iqbal, Ivan Vulić, Anna Korhonen,\nL866@P12: and Sercan Ö Arık. Multi-agent design: Optimizing agents with better prompts and topologies.\nL867@P12: arXiv preprint arXiv:2502.02533, 2025.\nL868@P12-13: 13A Appendix Overview\nL869@P13: The appendix is structured as follows:\nL870@P13: • Section B discusses the limitations of MasFACT and outlines future research directions.\nL871@P13: • Section C provides the broader impact statement of this work.\nL872@P13: • Section D presents theoretical and implementation details of MasFACT, including the stochastic\nL873@P13: topology posterior, FGW alignment, PAC-Bayes transfer bound, training objective, prior-bank\nL874@P13: update, read-only evaluation protocol, and full algorithm.\nL875@P13: • Section E describes the hierarchical continual MAS evaluation protocol, including dataset organiza\u0002tion, continual scenario construction, evaluation metrics, and data usage.\nL876@P13: • Section F details the compared baselines, including single-agent methods, fixed MAS topologies,\nL877@P13: adaptive MAS topology methods, replay-enhanced variants, and plug-and-play MasFACT variants.\nL878@P13: • Section G reports additional experiments and analyses, including hyperparameter sensitivity,\nL879@P13: plug-and-play integration details, and complete experimental results.\nL880@P13: • Section H summarizes experimental reproducibility details, including released artifacts, evaluation\nL881@P13: protocol, few-shot settings, metrics, random seeds, hyperparameters, and compute resources.\nL882@P13: B Limitations and Future Work\nL883@P13: Although MasFACT improves continual MAS topology learning by explicitly preserving and\nL884@P13: transferring reusable communication structures, several limitations remain. First, the effectiveness of\nL885@P13: topology transfer depends on the quality of historical prior atoms. When early-stage topologies are\nL886@P13: noisy, low-utility, or insufficiently diverse, the prior bank may provide weak structural anchors for\nL887@P13: later tasks. Second, FGW-based alignment assumes that agent attributes and communication relations\nL888@P13: provide meaningful semantic and structural signals. In tasks where agent roles are poorly specified,\nL889@P13: highly ambiguous, or rapidly changing, the alignment cost may become less reliable.\nL890@P13: Future work will explore uncertainty-aware prior construction, adaptive alignment objectives, and\nL891@P13: more expressive agent-attribute modeling to improve robustness under noisy or weakly structured\nL892@P13: task streams. Another promising direction is to jointly study topology continual learning and\nL893@P13: agent capability adaptation, so that communication structures and agent-level reasoning behaviors\nL894@P13: can co-evolve under explicit stability constraints. Extending the evaluation protocol to interactive\nL895@P13: environments, embodied multi-agent tasks, and real-world tool-augmented workflows would further\nL896@P13: test whether continual topology transfer remains effective under more dynamic and open-ended\nL897@P13: conditions.\nL898@P13: C Impact Statements\nL899@P13: This work contributes to the study of continual learning in multi-agent systems by shifting attention from\nL900@P13: parameter preservation alone to the preservation, transfer, and adaptation of communication topology.\nL901@P13: The proposed MasFACT framework may benefit applications where multi-agent collaboration must\nL902@P13: adapt over time, such as mathematical reasoning, code generation, knowledge-intensive question\nL903@P13: answering, scientific problem solving, and tool-augmented decision support. By reusing validated\nL904@P13: historical topology priors, the method may reduce repeated topology search and improve the efficiency\nL905@P13: and stability of MAS deployment under evolving task distributions.\nL906@P13: The hierarchical continual MAS evaluation protocol introduced in this work may also help future\nL907@P13: research more systematically assess topology-level forgetting, structural transfer, and long-horizon\nL908@P13: collaboration robustness across heterogeneous reasoning tasks. Since our experiments are conducted\nL909@P13: on public benchmark datasets and focus on methodological evaluation, we do not expect direct\nL910@P13: privacy risks from the reported study. However, stronger and more adaptive MAS frameworks\nL911@P13: may also be used in high-stakes or automated decision-making contexts. In such settings, topology\nL912@P13: adaptation should be paired with careful human oversight, transparent logging of agent interactions,\nL913@P13: and task-specific safety constraints to prevent unreliable or harmful automated behavior.\nL914@P13-14: 14D Theoretical and Implementation Details of MasFACT\nL915@P14: This appendix provides technical details omitted from the main text. We first define the masked\nL916@P14: stochastic topology posterior used by MasFACT. We then expand the FGW alignment objective and\nL917@P14: the transport-induced projection used to push historical prior atoms into the current agent space. Next,\nL918@P14: we state the risk assumptions and prove the geometry-aware PAC-Bayes transfer bound in Theorem 1.\nL919@P14: Finally, we describe the bound-induced training surrogate, selective prior-bank update, read-only\nL920@P14: evaluation protocol, and full algorithm.\nL921@P14: D.1 Stochastic Topology Posterior\nL922@P14: For task Tt, let Ct be the aligned prior center obtained after FGW retrieval and projection, and let Rt\nL923@P14: be the task-specific residual score matrix. The prior and posterior in the main text are defined through\nL924@P14: a masked stochastic mapping over the feasible topology space Bt. For a generic score matrix Z, we\nL925@P14: define\nL926@P14: Π\nL927@P14: stoch\nL928@P14: Bt\nL929@P14: (B; Z) = exp (⟨Z, B⟩)I{B ∈ Bt}\nL930@P14: P\nL931@P14: B′∈Bt\nL932@P14: exp (⟨Z, B′⟩)\nL933@P14: . (11)\nL934@P14: Thus, the FGW-pushed prior and residual posterior are\nL935@P14: pt(B) = Πstoch\nL936@P14: Bt\nL937@P14: (B; Ct), qt(B) = Πstoch\nL938@P14: Bt\nL939@P14: (B; Ct + Rt). (12)\nL940@P14: The feasible-space mask prevents invalid communication graphs from receiving probability mass.\nL941@P14: Equation 11 gives the normalized posterior distribution used for PAC-Bayes analysis. In implementa\u0002tion, we use a masked straight-through Gumbel-Sigmoid relaxation to sample valid communication\nL942@P14: graphs while preserving differentiability with respect to Rt. During execution, MasFACT selects a\nL943@P14: high-probability topology\nL944@P14: Bexec\nL945@P14: t = arg max\nL946@P14: B∈Bt\nL947@P14: qt(B). (13)\n----------------------------------------------------------------------------