 For posterior qt, the true and\nL1049@P15: empirical topology risks are\nL1050@P15: Rt(qt) = EB∼qtEz∼Dt[ℓt(B; z)] , RbSt(qt) = EB∼qt\nL1051@P15: 1\nL1052@P15: st\nL1053@P15: X\nL1054@P15: z∈St\nL1055@P15: ℓt(B; z). (25)\nL1056@P15: The empirical risk RbSt(qt) is the loss-form counterpart of the utility-based topology objective in\nL1057@P15: Section 2.\nL1058@P15: Bounded loss. For any topology B and any sample z ∼ Dt, the normalized topology-induced loss\nL1059@P15: satisfies ℓt(B; z) ∈ [0, 1].\nL1060@P15: Prior independence from empirical topology loss. The prior bank M is built from previous tasks.\nL1061@P15: For the current task, retrieval uses the current scaffold graph, agent attributes, and historical atom\nL1062@P15: statistics, but not the empirical topology loss used to optimize qt on St. Therefore, conditional on\nL1063@P15: the task-side scaffold information, the selected prior is independent of the empirical support loss\nL1064@P15: appearing in RbSt(qt).\nL1065@P15: Local FGW stability. Within the conservative adaptation neighborhood around the aligned prior,\nL1066@P15: the expected topology risk is locally stable under FGW perturbations. This assumption does not\nL1067@P15: require individual LLM responses to be continuous under arbitrary graph edits. It only states that\nL1068@P15: the expected MAS performance under a fixed evaluation protocol varies smoothly for small FGW\nL1069@P15: perturbations around the aligned prior. Under this local stability, risk distortion caused by imperfectly\nL1070@P15: transporting a historical prior into the current task space is bounded by cAAt.\nL1071@P15: Prior compression stability. Each shared prior atom summarizes a cluster of historical high-utility\nL1072@P15: topologies. Let σt be the structural dispersion of the selected atom. The uncertainty introduced by\nL1073@P15: compressing that historical topology cluster into a single atom is bounded by cσσt. A smaller σt\nL1074@P15: indicates a more stable and reliable historical prior.\nL1075@P15: D.4 Proof of the Geometry-Aware PAC-Bayes Transfer Bound\nL1076@P15: We first recall a standard PAC-Bayes bound for a fixed prior.\nL1077@P15-16: 16Lemma 1: Fixed-prior PAC-Bayes bound. Let p be a prior distribution over topology graphs that\nL1078@P16: is independent of the empirical support loss on St. Under bounded loss, for any δ ∈ (0, 1), with\nL1079@P16: probability at least 1 − δ over the draw of St, for all topology posteriors qt,\nL1080@P16: Rt(qt) ≤ RbSt(qt) + s\nL1081@P16: DKL(qt∥p) + log 2\nL1082@P16: √st\nL1083@P16: δ\nL1084@P16: 2st\nL1085@P16: . (26)\nL1086@P16: This is the McAllester-style PAC-Bayes bound for bounded losses.\nL1087@P16: Lemma 2: Finite prior-bank selection. Let M be a finite prior bank independent of the empirical\nL1088@P16: support loss on St. For each atom Hm ∈ M, let pt,m denote the corresponding task-side prior after\nL1089@P16: FGW alignment. With probability at least 1 − δ, for all m ∈ [M] and all topology posteriors qt,\nL1090@P16: Rt(qt) ≤ RbSt(qt) + s\nL1091@P16: DKL(qt∥pt,m) + log 2M\nL1092@P16: √st\nL1093@P16: δ\nL1094@P16: 2st\nL1095@P16: . (27)\nL1096@P16: Proof of Lemma 2. Apply Lemma 1 to each candidate prior pt,m with confidence level δ/M. For a\nL1097@P16: fixed m, the logarithmic term becomes\nL1098@P16: log\nL1099@P16: 2\nL1100@P16: √\nL1101@P16: st\nL1102@P16: δ/M = log\nL1103@P16: 2M\nL1104@P16: √\nL1105@P16: st\nL1106@P16: δ\nL1107@P16: . (28)\nL1108@P16: A union bound over all m ∈ [M] yields Eq. 27. The factor log M comes from finite prior-bank\nL1109@P16: selection, rather than unrestricted data-dependent prior search.\nL1110@P16: Proof of Theorem 1. Let pt = pt,mt be the selected FGW-pushed prior. By Lemma 2, the\nL1111@P16: PAC-Bayes bound holds for this selected prior because prior retrieval does not use the empirical\nL1112@P16: topology loss on St. The local FGW stability assumption adds the alignment correction cAAt, and\nL1113@P16: the prior compression stability assumption adds the compression correction cσσt. Combining the\nL1114@P16: terms gives\nL1115@P16: Rt(qt) ≤ RbSt(qt) + s\nL1116@P16: DKL(qt∥pt) + log 2M\nL1117@P16: √st\nL1118@P16: δ\nL1119@P16: 2st\nL1120@P16: + cAAt + cσσt, (29)\nL1121@P16: which proves Theorem 1. This is a conservative transfer bound: the additional terms explicitly expose\nL1122@P16: the quality of FGW alignment and historical prior compression.\nL1123@P16: D.5 Bound-Induced Training Objective\nL1124@P16: Theorem 1 motivates optimizing an empirical surrogate of the upper bound. The first term is the\nL1125@P16: empirical support risk RbSt(qt). The second term penalizes posterior-prior complexity around the\nL1126@P16: FGW-pushed prior. In practice, exact enumeration over Bt can be expensive, so the KL term is\nL1127@P16: estimated through the masked stochastic relaxation used by Πstoch\nL1128@P16: Bt\nL1129@P16: . The residual sparsity penalty\nL1130@P16: further encourages local topology edits around the aligned center. This yields the main training\nL1131@P16: objective:\nL1132@P16: Ltrain = RbSt(qt) + λKLDKL(qt∥pt) + λR∥Rt∥1. (30)\nL1133@P16: The KL term controls drift from the aligned prior, while the ℓ1 term restricts residual adaptation to\nL1134@P16: sparse edge corrections. The constants cA and cσ in the theorem are not optimization hyperparameters;\nL1135@P16: they characterize the sensitivity of expected risk to alignment distortion and prior compression.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMasFACT: Continual Multi-Agent Topology Learning via Geometry-Aware Posterior Transfer (https://arxiv.org/pdf/2605.17361v1)\nciteturn41152view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 29; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn41146view0\",\"lineno\":1178}); Total lines: 1757\nL1127@P16: estimated through the masked stochastic relaxation used by Πstoch\nL1128@P16: Bt\nL1129@P16: . The residual sparsity penalty\nL1130@P16: further encourages local topology edits around the aligned center. This yields the main training\nL1131@P16: objective:\nL1132@P16: Ltrain = RbSt(qt) + λKLDKL(qt∥pt) + λR∥Rt∥1. (30)\nL1133@P16: The KL term controls drift from the aligned prior, while the ℓ1 term restricts residual adaptation to\nL1134@P16: sparse edge corrections. The constants cA and cσ in the theorem are not optimization hyperparameters;\nL1135@P16: they characterize the sensitivity of expected risk to alignment distortion and prior compression.\nL1136@P16: D.6 Selective Prior-Bank Update\nL1137@P16: After adapting to task Tt, MasFACT updates the prior bank only with validated posterior topology\nL1138@P16: evidence. Let Bexec\nL1139@P16: t = arg maxB∈Bt\nL1140@P16: qt(B) be the execution topology, and let\nL1141@P16: Bt = EB∼qt\nL1142@P16: [B] (31)\nL1143@P16: be the posterior structural summary. The execution topology is used to evaluate validation utility,\nL1144@P16: while the posterior summary is used as stable evidence for memory update.\nL1145@P16-17: 17We define the transfer complexity used for memory gating as\nL1146@P17: κt = DKL(qt∥pt) + cAAt + cσσt. (32)\nL1147@P17: This quantity mirrors the non-empirical complexity terms in Theorem 1. A posterior is considered\nL1148@P17: reliable for memory update only when UbSt(Bexec\nL1149@P17: t\nL1150@P17: ) ≥ τU . If additionally κt ≤ τκ, the evidence is\nL1151@P17: absorbed into the retrieved atom. If UbSt\nL1152@P17: (Bexec\nL1153@P17: t\nL1154@P17: ) ≥ τU but κt > τκ, the evidence initializes a new\nL1155@P17: prior atom, indicating a high-utility collaboration pattern not well explained by the current bank.\nL1156@P17: For atom refinement, the posterior summary is transported back to the selected atom space:\nL1157@P17: Set = QtBtQ⊤\nL1158@P17: t\nL1159@P17: . (33)\nL1160@P17: The selected atom is then updated by exponential moving average:\nL1161@P17: Smt ← (1 − ηt)Smt + ηtSet, U¯mt ← (1 − ηt)U¯mt + ηtUbSt\nL1162@P17: (Bexec\nL1163@P17: t\nL1164@P17: ). (34)\nL1165@P17: The dispersion statistic σmtis updated by the moving average of the FGW deviation between the new\nL1166@P17: evidence and the updated atom center. The update weight ηt is fixed by the validation protocol and is\nL1167@P17: not tuned on test tasks.\nL1168@P17: For memory expansion, MasFACT initializes a new atom using the validated posterior summary in\nL1169@P17: the current task space:\nL1170@P17: Hnew = (Bt, Xt, µt), U¯\nL1171@P17: new = UbSt\nL1172@P17: (Bexec\nL1173@P17: t\nL1174@P17: ), σnew = 0. (35)\nL1175@P17: This mechanism prevents high-utility but structurally novel collaboration patterns from being forced\nL1176@P17: into an incompatible historical atom. In implementation, the prior-bank size is controlled by retaining\nL1177@P17: atoms with high historical utility and low dispersion.\nL1178@P17: D.7 Read-only Evaluation Protocol\nL1179@P17: For old-task evaluation, MasFACT operates in read-only mode. The system retrieves and aligns\nL1180@P17: stored topology priors, forms the inference posterior, and executes a high-probability topology\nL1181@P17: under that posterior. No gradient update, posterior consolidation, or prior-bank writing is allowed.\nL1182@P17: This protocol ensures that old-task performance reflects topology memory reuse rather than hidden\nL1183@P17: test-time relearning or replay.\nL1184@P17: D.8 Algorithm Details\nL1185@P17: This section presents the detailed algorithmic procedure of our proposed method, MasFACT, which\nL1186@P17: encompasses three stages: (1) a base stage for training a topology generator on a data-rich initial\nL1187@P17: task and initializing a factorized topology prior bank; (2) a continual adaptation stage that iteratively\nL1188@P17: retrieves, aligns, and refines topological priors to construct task-adapted execution topologies while\nL1189@P17: dynamically expanding the prior bank based on utility and transfer-complexity thresholds; and (3) a\nL1190@P17: read-only evaluation stage that leverages stored priors for inference without further parameter updates\nL1191@P17: or memory modification. Algorithmic details are provided in Algorithm 1.\nL1192@P17: E Details of the Hierarchical Continual MAS Evaluation Protocol\nL1193@P17: This appendix provides details of the datasets and task construction used in our hierarchical continual\nL1194@P17: MAS evaluation protocol. The goal of this protocol is not to introduce new raw datasets, but to\nL1195@P17: reorganize existing high-quality reasoning benchmarks into continual MAS learning scenarios. The\nL1196@P17: protocol is designed to evaluate whether a MAS can maintain strong system-level performance when\nL1197@P17: task requirements, data distributions, reasoning styles, and subskills evolve over time.\nL1198@P17: E.1 Overview of Dataset Organization\nL1199@P17: We organize 17 datasets into four reasoning families: knowledge QA, mathematical reasoning,\nL1200@P17: code generation, and knowledge-intensive multi-hop reasoning. These families are selected because\nL1201@P17: they induce different collaboration requirements among agents. Knowledge QA emphasizes answer\nL1202@P17: verification and conflict resolution; mathematical reasoning emphasizes decomposition and step-wise\nL1203@P17-18: 18Algorithm 1 MasFACT Continual Training and Read-only Evaluation\nL1204@P18: Require: Task sequence {T0, . . . , TK}; support sets {St}\nL1205@P18: K\nL1206@P18: t=1; utility threshold τU ; transfer\u0002complexity threshold τκ\nL1207@P18: Ensure: Task-adapted execution topologies {Bexec\nL1208@P18: t }\nL1209@P18: K\nL1210@P18: t=0 and topology prior bank M\nL1211@P18: 1: Base Stage\nL1212@P18: 2: Train the topology generator on the data-rich base task T0\nL1213@P18: 3: Collect high-utility topologies from T0\nL1214@P18: 4: Initialize topology prior bank M with factorized prior atoms\nL1215@P18: 5: Continual Adaptation Stage\nL1216@P18: 6: for t = 1, . . . , K do\nL1217@P18: 7: Construct current scaffold graph G\nL1218@P18: 0\nL1219@P18: t = (B0\nL1220@P18: t\nL1221@P18: , Xt, µt)\nL1222@P18: 8: for each prior atom Hm ∈ M do\nL1223@P18: 9: Compute FGW coupling T\nL1224@P18: ∗\nL1225@P18: t,m between G\nL1226@P18: 0\nL1227@P18: t\nL1228@P18: and Hm\nL1229@P18: 10: Compute alignment cost At,m = DFGW(G\nL1230@P18: 0\nL1231@P18: t\nL1232@P18: , Hm; T\nL1233@P18: ∗\nL1234@P18: t,m)\nL1235@P18: 11: end for\nL1236@P18: 12: Retrieve prior atom Hmtusing Eq. 6\nL1237@P18: 13: Project Smtinto the current agent space to obtain Ct\nL1238@P18: 14: Define prior pt(B) = Πstoch\nL1239@P18: Bt\nL1240@P18: (B; Ct)\nL1241@P18: 15: Form posterior qt(B) = Πstoch\nL1242@P18: Bt\nL1243@P18: (B; Ct + Rt)\nL1244@P18: 16: Optimize Ltrain in Eq. 10\nL1245@P18: 17: Select execution topology Bexec\nL1246@P18: t = arg maxB∈Bt\nL1247@P18: qt(B)\nL1248@P18: 18: Compute κt = DKL(qt∥pt) + cAAt + cσσt\nL1249@P18: 19: if UbSt(Bexec\nL1250@P18: t\nL1251@P18: ) ≥ τU and κt ≤ τκ then\nL1252@P18: 20: Update atom Hmt with validated posterior statistics\nL1253@P18: 21: else if UbSt(Bexec\nL1254@P18: t\nL1255@P18: ) ≥ τU and κt > τκ then\nL1256@P18: 22: Initialize a new atom with validated posterior statistics\nL1257@P18: 23: end if\nL1258@P18: 24: end for\nL1259@P18: 25: Read-only Evaluation Stage\nL1260@P18: 26: for each seen task Ti, i ≤ K do\nL1261@P18: 27: Retrieve and align stored topology priors\nL1262@P18: 28: Generate task-compatible topology from the inference posterior\nL1263@P18: 29: Evaluate without gradient updates or memory writing\nL1264@P18: 30: end for\nL1265@P18: checking; code generation emphasizes generation, execution, debugging, and repair; and multi-hop\nL1266@P18: reasoning emphasizes evidence retrieval and aggregation.\nL1267@P18: Based on these datasets, we construct continual learning scenarios at three levels of task evolution:\nL1268@P18: • Task-level evolution: stages correspond to different reasoning families, forming a cross\u0002scenario continual learning sequence.\nL1269@P18: • Domain-level evolution: stages correspond to different datasets within the same reasoning\nL1270@P18: family, capturing distribution, difficulty, and evaluation-style shifts.\nL1271@P18: • Class-level evolution: stages correspond to coarse- or fine-grained subcategories inside a\nL1272@P18: representative benchmark, capturing subskill evolution and long-horizon continual learning.\nL1273@P18: This design yields 12 continual MAS scenarios: one task-level sequence, four domain-level sequences,\nL1274@P18: four coarse-grained class-level sequences, and three fine-grained class-level sequences.\nL1275@P18: E.2 Knowledge QA Datasets\n"
